em.\n\nBut the problem's examples show that the numbers are in descending order, so the function should return them sorted that way.\n\nSo, the steps are:\n\n- collect all the i's as per the mask.\n\n- sort them in reverse order.\n\n- return as a list.\n\nBut for N=5, the mask generates 0,1,4,5. Then sorted reverse is 5,4,1,0. Which matches the example.\n\nSo the code would work.\n\nNow, let's test this approach with another example. Let's take N=9 (binary 1001). The set bits are at positions 0 and 3. So m=2.\n\nmask ranges from 0 to 3.\n\nmask 0: 0 \u2192 0.\n\nmask 1: include position 0 \u2192 1.\n\nmask 2: include position 3 \u2192 8.\n\nmask3: include both \u2192 9.\n\nSo the list is [0,1,8,9]. Sorted in reverse: 9,8,1,0. Which matches the example.\n\nAnother test case: N=3 (binary 11). Positions are 0 and 1. mask 0: 0, 1:1, 2:2,3:3. sorted reverse is 3,2,1,0. Output would be [3,2,1,0].\n\nSo this approach works.\n\nNow, the code:\n\nFirst, collect the set bits positions.\n\nThen generate all masks, compute the sum.\n\nThen sort in reverse order.\n\nNow, code in Python.\n\nBut the function is supposed to take N as input and return the list.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\nclass Solution:\n\n    def printSubsets(self, N):\n\n        # collect positions of set bits\n\n        positions = []\n\n        current_bit = 0\n\n        temp = N\n\n        while temp > 0:\n\n            if temp & 1:\n\n                positions.append(current_bit)\n\n            temp >>= 1\n\n            current_bit +=1\n\n        m = len(positions)\n\n        res = []\n\n        for mask in range(1 << m):\n\n            current = 0\n\n            for j in range(m):\n\n                if mask & (1 << j):\n\n                    current += (1 << positions[j])\n\n            res.append(current)\n\n        # sort in descending order\n\n        res.sort(reverse=True)\n\n        return res\n\nBut wait, the problem statement says that the output is 5 4 1 0 for N=5. So in the function, when we return 