s can be covered by one of the lines formed by pairs of these three points.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(input[0])\n    points = []\n    idx = 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        x = int(input[idx])\n        y = int(input[idx + 1])\n        points.append((x, y))\n        idx += 2\n\n    if n <= 2:\n        print(\"success\")\n        return\n\n    def is_colinear(points_list):\n        if len(points_list) <= 2:\n            return True\n        (ax, ay), (bx, by) = points_list[:2]\n        for (x, y) in points_list[2:]:\n            if (bx - ax) * (y - ay) != (by - ay) * (x - ax):\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    if is_colinear(points):\n        print(\"success\")\n        return\n\n    a, b, c = points[0], points[1], points[2]\n\n    def check_line(p1, p2):\n        p1x, p1y = p1\n        p2x, p2y = p2\n        others = []\n        for (x, y) in points:\n            if (p2x - p1x) * (y - p1y) != (p2y - p1y) * (x - p1x):\n                others.append((x, y))\n        if len(others) <= 1:\n            return True\n        (ox, oy), (ox2, oy2) = others[:2]\n        for (x, y) in others[2:]:\n            if (ox2 - ox) * (y - oy) != (oy2 - oy) * (x - ox):\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    if check_line(a, b) or check_line(a, c) or check_line(b, c):\n        print(\"success\")\n    else:\n        print(\"failure\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input**: The input is read and parsed into a list of points.\n2. **Trivial Cases Handling**: Directly return \"success\" for 0, 1, or 2 points.\n3. **Collinearity Check**: Check if all points lie on a single line using the cross-product method to avoid floating-point inaccuracies.\n4. **Three Lines Check**: If the points are not all collinear, check each of the three lines formed by the first three non-collinear points. For each line, collect points no