            available[c] -= 1\n            continue\n        \n        # While we can remove the last character from the stack to make room for a larger one\n        while stack and stack[-1] > c:\n            last_char = stack[-1]\n            # Check if we have enough remaining instances of the last character to fulfill its required count\n            # Also, ensure we're not exceeding the required count if we remove it\n            if required[last_char] < available[last_char]:\n                # We can safely remove it\n                stack.pop()\n                required[last_char] += 1\n            else:\n                break  # Can't remove, break the loop\n        \n        # Add current character to the stack and decrement the required count\n        stack.append(c)\n        required[c] -= 1\n        available[c] -= 1\n\n    # The stack now holds the lexicographically largest possible subsequence meeting the required counts\n    # Reverse it to get the lexicographically smallest A\n    return ''.join(stack[::-1])\n\n# Read input from stdin and print the result\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import sys\n    input_str = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    print(reverseShuffleMerge(input_str))\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Character Count Calculation**: We first determine how many times each character should appear in \\( A \\) by halving their counts in \\( S \\).\n2. **Greedy Algorithm**: We iterate through each character in \\( S \\) and use a stack to build the largest possible subsequence. For each character, if it can replace a previous character in the stack to form a larger subsequence while still meeting the required counts, we do so. This ensures we build the largest possible subsequence.\n3. **Reverse the Subsequence**: The largest subsequence is reversed to obtain the lexicographically smallest string \\( A \\), ensuring that \\( reverse(A) \\) is a valid subsequence of \\( S \\) and the remaining characters can form a permutation of \\( A \\).\n\n